WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.042 --> 00:00:01.376
Today,
I'm going to read a passage

00:00:01.460 --> 00:00:02.336
from the book Munich.

00:00:02.419 --> 00:00:03.879
By Robert Harris.

00:00:03.962 --> 00:00:06.548
Legat took out a large,
white cotton handkerchief

00:00:06.632 --> 00:00:07.549
and wiped his face.

00:00:07.633 --> 00:00:08.467
It would not do

00:00:08.550 --> 00:00:10.177
to turn up red faced
and perspiring.

00:00:10.260 --> 00:00:12.137
If there was one sin
that was frowned upon

00:00:12.221 --> 00:00:14.223
above all others
in the private office,

00:00:14.306 --> 00:00:15.724
it was appearing
to be in a flap.

00:00:15.807 --> 00:00:17.976
He climbed the steps
into the narrow, shadowed,

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.229
soot-black, and cutting
of Downing Street.

00:00:20.312 --> 00:00:21.939
On the pavement
opposite Number 10,

00:00:22.022 --> 00:00:23.607
a group of reporters
turned their heads

00:00:23.690 --> 00:00:24.691
to follow his arrival.

00:00:24.775 --> 00:00:26.860
A photographer
raised his camera,

00:00:26.944 --> 00:00:28.820
but when he saw
it was no one of importance,

00:00:28.904 --> 00:00:30.155
he lowered it again.

00:00:30.239 --> 00:00:32.032
Legat nodded
to the policeman

00:00:32.115 --> 00:00:34.284
who wrapped once hard
with the knocker.

00:00:34.368 --> 00:00:36.286
The door opened
as if of its own volition.

00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:37.663
He stepped inside.

00:00:37.746 --> 00:00:38.622
It was four months

00:00:38.705 --> 00:00:40.624
since he had been seconded
from the foreign office

00:00:40.707 --> 00:00:41.792
to work in Number 10,

00:00:41.875 --> 00:00:44.169
yet each time
he felt that same sensation

00:00:44.253 --> 00:00:46.839
as if he was entering
some gentleman's club

00:00:46.922 --> 00:00:48.382
that was no longer
fashionable.

00:00:48.465 --> 00:00:50.092
The black
and white-tiled lobby,

00:00:50.175 --> 00:00:51.885
the walls of Pompeiian red,

00:00:51.969 --> 00:00:53.846
the brass lantern,
the grandfather clock

00:00:53.929 --> 00:00:55.472
ticking
its leisurely heartbeat.

00:00:55.556 --> 00:00:57.182
Somewhere in the depths
of the building,

00:00:57.766 --> 00:00:58.684
a telephone rang.

00:00:58.767 --> 00:01:00.769
The doorkeeper wished Legat
a good afternoon

00:01:00.853 --> 00:01:02.563
and returned to
his leather coachman's seat

00:01:02.646 --> 00:01:04.189
and his copy
of the Evening Standard.

00:01:04.773 --> 00:01:05.816
In the wide passageway

00:01:05.899 --> 00:01:07.442
leading
to the back of the building,

00:01:07.526 --> 00:01:10.112
Legat paused and checked
himself in the mirror.

00:01:10.195 --> 00:01:11.113
He straightened his tie

00:01:11.196 --> 00:01:13.115
and smoothed down his hair
with both hands.

00:01:14.032 --> 00:01:16.535
He braced his shoulders
and turned.

00:01:16.618 --> 00:01:18.161
Ahead of him
was the cabinet room,

00:01:18.245 --> 00:01:19.496
its panelled door closed.

00:01:20.205 --> 00:01:21.206
To his left,

00:01:21.290 --> 00:01:23.208
the office
used by Sir Horace Wilson,

00:01:23.292 --> 00:01:24.251
also closed.

00:01:24.877 --> 00:01:27.379
To his right, the corridor
that led to the offices

00:01:27.462 --> 00:01:29.673
of the prime minister's
private secretaries.

00:01:29.756 --> 00:01:30.591
The Georgian house

00:01:30.674 --> 00:01:32.801
exuded an air
of imperturbable calm.

00:01:33.343 --> 00:01:36.722
Chamberlain took his seat
behind the microphone

00:01:37.222 --> 00:01:39.183
and spread out his speech.

00:01:39.266 --> 00:01:40.893
His hands were shaking.

00:01:41.643 --> 00:01:43.645
One of the pages
fell to the floor

00:01:43.729 --> 00:01:46.815
and he had to bend stiffly
to retrieve it.

00:01:47.316 --> 00:01:48.358
He muttered

00:01:49.318 --> 00:01:51.236
and wobbling
all over the place.

00:01:52.362 --> 00:01:53.864
He asked
for a glass of water.

00:01:54.781 --> 00:01:56.033
Legat poured one

00:01:56.116 --> 00:01:58.202
from the jug
in the centre of the table.

00:01:58.285 --> 00:02:01.330
In his anxiety,
he overfilled it.

00:02:01.413 --> 00:02:05.042
Beads of water stood out
on the polished surface.

00:02:05.125 --> 00:02:07.836
The BBC engineer
asked them all to sit

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:09.296
at the other end
of the room.

00:02:10.005 --> 00:02:13.592
Outside, over the garden
and horse guards parade,

00:02:14.134 --> 00:02:15.969
darkness had fallen.

00:02:17.304 --> 00:02:19.932
Big Ben
chimed eight o'clock.

00:02:20.015 --> 00:02:22.643
The announcers voice
came over the loud speaker.

00:02:22.726 --> 00:02:24.603
This is London.

00:02:24.686 --> 00:02:25.771
In a moment,

00:02:25.854 --> 00:02:27.856
you will hear
the prime minister,

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:30.359
the Right Honourable
Neville Chamberlain,

00:02:30.442 --> 00:02:33.612
speaking
from Number 10 Downing St.

00:02:33.695 --> 00:02:36.949
His speech will be heard
all over the empire,

00:02:37.032 --> 00:02:39.201
throughout the continent
of America,

00:02:40.452 --> 00:02:42.829
and in a large number
of foreign countries.

00:02:43.330 --> 00:02:44.706
Mr Chamberlain.

00:02:46.333 --> 00:02:49.419
Beside the microphone,
a green light glowed.

00:02:50.879 --> 00:02:54.091
The prime minister
adjusted his cuffs

00:02:54.174 --> 00:02:55.884
and picked up his speech.

00:02:55.968 --> 00:02:58.470
I want to say
a few words to you,

00:02:58.554 --> 00:03:01.098
men and women of Britain
and the empire.

00:03:01.181 --> 00:03:04.643
And perhaps,
to others as well.

00:03:05.435 --> 00:03:09.022
His tone was euphonious,
melancholy,

00:03:09.106 --> 00:03:12.067
as inspiring as a dirge.

00:03:14.736 --> 00:03:16.488
-Slam. All you'll get.
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:03:16.572 --> 00:03:17.698
That's all you'll get.

